now since boris johnson's resignation  all attention has focused on mps in  westminster and the ballots that have  seen the leadership candidates whittle  down to the final two now the power to  pick the next pm moves out across the  country to the conservative party  membership who will choose the next  leader so who exactly does that mean is  making the final decision  this research from 2020 shows how  conservative members are spread over the  various age groups 39 of them are over  65 compared to 19  in the whole uk population  the majority of conservative members are  male well nationally the distribution is  fairly equal a 2017 survey found that  members ethnicity was 97 white british  compared to 78 of the population in  england and wales  and most of the members live in the  south of england with only six percent  in scotland  so let's discuss it we're joined now by  lauren mckevitt a former government  special adviser who's not yet decided  who to vote for claire pearsall a  conservative councillor and former party  advisor who had been backing penny  morgan but will now switch to rishi soon  again alex dean public affairs  consultant and former conservative  advisor he's backing liz trust lauren  claire and alex thanks so much for  talking to us um alex let's start with  you  why liz truss  well for me and like to be clear i was  backing kevin vadenk until she was  knocked out you can't always get what  you want um i support liz trust because  of these two candidates i see her as  more of the free market  small state low tax kind of conservatism  i want that may not flow to everybody's  boat but it certainly floats mine and  rishi sunak whilst he faced of course  the challenges of coronavirus and if  he's prime minister i'd still be happy  i'd prefer liz trust because i see her  as more in the thatcherite mould and i  see her as somebody who's going to take  less of a kind of brownite approach  without money i suppose oh that's  interesting isn't it and we'll get back  to the whole issue of who's that's right  and who isn't but claire let's turn to  you now i was having a read through my  notes and it said that um you do find it  depressing but you will vote for rishi  sunak  yes that's right um i  started out actually supporting tom  tugan had because i thought that he  would offer a clean fresh start that we  all needed uh but then changed over to  penny morton so to have both of your  candidates your preferred choices to be  knocked out leaves you with the choice  between these two which isn't my ideal  and i'll be completely upfront about  that  and i think i've settled on rishi soon  act currently  because looking at the finances i know  liz trust is promising a lot of tax cuts  which everybody would like  but rishi does make the point that where  do we get the money from to support the  country you can't just keep cutting  taxes you do need to get that money from  somewhere and being fiscally competent  is what a conservative is all about  it's interesting you say and we will get  to this again we'll come back um to you  and specifically about  who who your ideal would be but in a  moment um so lauren  that's who they are going to vote for  that's who they back um you're undecided  yes well i didn't i decided not to try  and fall too heavily into one camp while  a whole other electorate of people was  narrowing the field down to two i  thought that all the candidates in the  field said some interesting things and  some slightly batty things um and i  think that the the race between rishi  and liz will be an interesting one i  find it interesting that alex is um  thinking that liz is more in the  thatcherite mould when in fact uh as  rishi pointed out in one of his hostings  uh his his husting's answers um liz is  uh of the two of them the only one who's  in favor really of looking at long-term  debt of war bond options and for the  possibility of offsetting against tax  cuts which is a more left-wing approach  to balancing the economy um  and i think that we're going to have a  very interesting debate over the next  six weeks the conservative party is one  of the oldest parties in the world and  we have reinvented ourselves more times  than all three of us can count and this  is an entirely new generation of member  of parliament running for the leadership  of our party and i really hope that what  that means is a very serious and great  debate about what direction our party  takes for the next 10 years um and so i  am happy to remain undecided and listen  to what the new generation would like to  debate  okay  so let's pick up on that thatcherite um  theme and go back to you alex i mean  sonic himself said my values are  thatcher right i believe in hard work  family and integrity and um your  candidate liz trust has said she isn't  um she has she's denied modeling herself  on margaret thatcher despite the t-shirt  the blouse excuse me and the bow we all  saw that um and she insists i am my own  person  yeah of course she's her own person one  seeking to read the runes on these  people as we were with the whole of the  candidate field to see who we think  most clearly reflects what we believe  and uh i think liz trust has a pretty  clear low tax agenda she spent some time  burnishing that in think tank land and  in her roles i liked very much what she  did on international trade to both the  rollover deals and the new deals but  look let's just say one thing and i  respect my two fellow panelists greatly  i would have chosen any of the people in  the field over uh kyostama and i think  any of them would have beaten kierst  dharma if selected so i know that some  people are looking at this and thinking  to themselves gosh the tory party it's  all blue on blue well yeah but on the  other hand we were very lucky to have a  field of talented candidates who i think  will take it to labour and whoever wins  is going to beat them my view is that  this is a reflection of the quality of  the candidates available in the tory  party bench that we have this kind of  conversation okay do you do you agree  that whether it's sonic or trust they  will both beat kierstamr  you're asking me yeah that's what i'm  sorry no no no i did say claire clay can  you hear me i can now yes  yes i yes i do think they can beat  kierstama i think that  one of the benefits we have as alex has  just pointed out is that our party has  an extreme amount of talent sitting  there what i do find a little  disappointing is putting out of a  fantasy cabinet list from both of the  top candidates this morning where there  are very very few women  and i would hope as lauren said that we  can reinvent ourselves to look at new  talent to look at women coming through  to make their voices heard around the  table what we don't want to see is  pretty much what we've had over the last  three years because in my mind that  hasn't worked so we need to change this  up and there is some exceptional talent  sitting there in either junior  ministerial or back bench positions that  need to be brought forward but nobody is  mentioning those at the moment so i  think we need to turn this round but i  do absolutely believe we can beat  kirsten armor  okay alice i just want to pick up on  what you said about increased borrowing  cutting taxes given that interest  payments on our national debt have hit  an all-time high new figures out this  morning do you still  back liz truss and think that increased  borrowing cutting taxes is not going to  increase inflation and it's where we  should be going  yes i do at the same time we've seen  plenty of economic studies saying that  the new chancellor will have and people  vary on their precise estimate but circa  60 billion of fiscal headroom to make  adjustments which are not inflationary i  know lauren suggested that this is a  left-wing approach to economics i mean  if it's left-wing it's also what ronald  reagan did so um it's an interesting  perspective on where the political  spectrum sits if you if you think that  that's uh left-wing i think that you we  should as a country be investing right  now to ensure that people don't get  another tax rise i think it's the worst  time to have national insurance hike  many times on your program kimberly  people are talking about the cost of  living crisis i think it's absolutely  the wrong time to be taxing people more  unfortunately electorally that's also  what people want they want their taxes  to be lower so it's useful if you can do  the right thing and a popular thing all  at the same time lauren when you're  going to decide looking at the next six  weeks what are the kind of things that  will sway you either way do you think  i think the thing that's going to sway  me the most is um what's coming in the  economy in the next six months obviously  both these candidates have the luxury of  campaigning where nobody in the country  needs to turn their heating on  we haven't reached the point of the  energy crisis in this country where  people are cold and i think the number  of people in work earning who are going  to be cold this winter is going to be  far higher than anybody is currently  assessing and that is something that if  either candidate backs  backs away from how they deal with that  then we're in real trouble and i'm  talking both about how we deal with that  in the immediate crisis terms and how we  deal with energy security and dealing  with environmental concerns such as  climate change in the coming years both  candidates have to address that very  seriously for me to begin to pay some  attention to them but where we come with  people who are in work and are feeling  the pinch more and more i don't think  either candidate is really talking  enough quite yet  about each of the tax bands and the way  that they impact upon people who are in  work and i'd like to see both of them  begin to talk more about that  and our role on the international stage  is going to be an incredibly important  one over the course of the next 10 years  because of our trading relationships and  because of how strong we as a country  have been in support of ukraine um and  both of them and how they outline their  international approach is going to be  something that i pay particular  attention to okay let's um let's look at  who's actually voting here right so  sonic doesn't really when it comes down  to it he just doesn't know the  membership like liz trusts us  well i would argue that liz trust  reaches a certain part of the membership  now where i live we fall into an older  demographic it's a  southeastern rural area  which does fall in largely into the 65  plus category as you showed previously  for membership and they are looking  quite hard at the finances of the  country but they're also looking at  which candidate is going to  look at the health service  that is a real issue especially in a  rural community you're looking at access  to gp surgeries you're looking at  hospital appointment availability and  transport  and neither candidate has mentioned that  at the moment so i'm not sure it's rishi  not understanding the demographics i  think he's just not talking in the  language at the moment that they want to  to hear so he needs to do a lot of work  on that liz trust will always always  have a base with a certain part of the  party but she equally then has to work  hard to to get over the sort of one  nation southern tory approach now we do  make up a large part portion of the  membership we can't be ignored just  because we're in the south east  so for her just to look at the red wall  seats at the moment will have a  detrimental effect on her campaign down  here in the south okay lauren claire and  alex thank you so much six weeks we'll  see how it goes thanks  thank you

